/**:
  ros__parameters:
    safety_distance: 0.40
    nav_safety_distance: 0.28
    turn_safety_distance: 0.25
    front_angle_deg: 40.0
    sector_angle_deg: 40.0
    lidar_yaw_offset_rad: 3.141592653589793
    lidar_ignore_below_m: 0.20
    ultrasonic_stop_m: 0.25
    use_lidar: true
    use_ultrasonic: true
    use_depth: true
    # ── Depth source, rebuilt 2026-09-21 (see plan 回写 14) ───────────────────
    #
    # ★ WHICH CAMERA. front_down, not front_up, and that is the whole feature.
    # Measured on 189 on 2026-09-21:
    #
    #   front_up   pitch +2.65 deg, 0.626 m up, z 1.639-3.699 m  (level camera)
    #   front_down pitch -27.17 deg, 0.311 m up, z 0.368-2.057 m (looking down)
    #
    # front_up is level, so its floor only enters the frame at 1.64 m, and any
    # fixed height band that excludes the floor must also exclude everything
    # shorter than ~0.27 m -- measured: admitting a 0.10 m obstacle at 1.64 m
    # needs y_max >= 0.66, while the floor's own y there starts at 0.666. About a
    # centimetre of separation, against centimetres of floor relief. So that band
    # only ever sees what the lidar already covers.
    #
    # front_down looks 27 deg down, so the floor is in view from 0.368 m out and
    # serves as a reference SURFACE instead of something to filter out. An
    # obstacle is then a point standing above that plane. Measured noise floor,
    # empty corridor: h > 0.020 -> 0.158% of points, h > 0.030 -> 0.020%,
    # h > 0.050 -> exactly zero. A 0.10 m box at 0.565 m gave 10545 points.
    #
    # (History, kept because the trap is still one parameter away. The original
    # implementation read /camera/front_up/depth/image_raw and took the minimum
    # over a CENTRED rectangle. front_up returns valid depth in only rows 320-479
    # (13.0% of the frame, z 1.285-2.607 m); rows 0-319 are exactly zero, on two
    # independent samples. A centred roi_frac 0.35 covers rows 156-323 and meets
    # that window on four rows, so `hits` never reached depth_min_hits and the
    # depth leg sat at "insufficient_hits" for the entire life of the feature --
    # the gate had only ever run on lidar + ultrasonics.)
    #
    # ★ Deliberately the RAW cloud, not xw_pc_nav_filter's points_nav output:
    # that filter caps its output at max_points_out (2500) after a voxel filter,
    # which can drop the very returns this test depends on, and it is shared with
    # the costmap, so its death would blind both at once.
    depth_pc_topic: /camera/front_down/depth/points
    depth_stop_m: 0.40
    #
    # ⚠️ SEMANTICS FLIPPED with the source. Under the image reading, "no usable
    # pixels" meant "I cannot see" and was distrusted. Here, 0 points standing
    # above the floor means "the corridor ahead is clear", which is the single
    # most trustworthy reading this camera produces. Distrust attaches to the
    # FRAME, and there are three distinct ways a frame can fail to inform:
    #   * no frame within depth_ttl_sec                -> stale
    #   * a frame arrived but no floor could be solved -> no_floor_model
    #   * the frame carries essentially no points      -> no_camera_data
    #
    # depth_raw_floor_points — a liveness floor, never a judgement threshold.
    # Measured 2026-09-21: the front_down cloud carries ~275000 valid points in a
    # corridor (of 307200 pixels), so 1000 is 0.4% of normal. It catches "the
    # camera chain is dead" and nothing subtler; depth_ttl_sec is the primary
    # defence.
    depth_raw_floor_points: 1000
    #
    # depth_min_points — how many points must stand above the floor before the
    # reading counts as "something is there" rather than "clear".
    #
    # ★★ DELIBERATELY 1. This is the code path, not a tuning choice.
    #
    #   In tick(), `depth_used` is assigned ONLY when `depth_state == "ok"`
    #   (line ~1085), and `depth_used` is in turn a PRECONDITION of the
    #   discrepancy test (line ~1109). So any value above a real obstacle's
    #   point count does not make the gate more conservative -- it makes the
    #   low-obstacle feature fail SILENTLY. That is the dangerous direction, and
    #   it is invisible: the gate keeps reporting `clear` and `safety_ok` while
    #   the sensor it is supposed to be acting on goes unread.
    #
    #   Noise does not need this guard, because noise is stopped by DISTANCE.
    #   Over a 1216-frame empty-corridor baseline (2026-09-21; 185 more frames in
    #   a second run) the nearest point standing above the floor was 1.455 m, so
    #   every noisy frame already fails `*depth_used < depth_low_obstacle_m`
    #   however many points it carries.
    #
    #   Measured 2026-09-21: a 0.10 m box gives 10545 points at 0.565 m and 3841
    #   at 0.907 m; empty-corridor noise reaches 449 points (p50 16, p90 111) but
    #   never closer than z = 1.24 m. An intermediate value such as 50 was tried
    #   and rejected: it splits the noise into 1..49 (`insufficient_points`,
    #   which then pollutes `degraded` in an empty corridor) and 50..449 (`ok`),
    #   while blinding the gate to any obstacle smaller than about 2 cm at 1 m.
    #   At 1, `insufficient_points` becomes unreachable and every real obstacle
    #   lands in `ok`; `clear` (no points at all, 85% of empty-corridor frames)
    #   stays the clean, trustworthy signal it is meant to be.
    depth_min_points: 1
    #
    # ── Floor-plane model of the front_down camera ───────────────────────────
    # The plane is y = a + b*z in the OPTICAL frame (Z forward, X right,
    # Y DOWN-POSITIVE). ★★ All six values below are CALIBRATED on 189 on
    # 2026-09-21 over 6+ independent frames; none is a guess.
    #
    # Measured plane: H = 0.3097..0.3112 m, pitch = -27.10..-27.19 deg.
    #   a0 = H / cos(pitch) = 0.3105 / cos(27.14 deg) = 0.3489
    #   b0 = -tan(pitch)    = -0.5126
    # ★ b0 is NEGATIVE and that is not a typo: Y is down-positive, so a camera
    #   pitched down sees the floor's y DECREASE with z. Sanity check -- at
    #   z = 0.368 m this plane gives y = +0.160, and the measured global y_max of
    #   the cloud is +0.162.
    depth_floor_a0: 0.3489
    depth_floor_b0: -0.5126
    #
    # depth_floor_h_min — how far above the plane a point must stand to count as
    # an obstacle. 0.05 sits at the measured noise floor with ~2x margin (see
    # above). Clamped to a minimum of 0.01 in code: at 0 the floor's own noise
    # crosses the test (measured p50 of h is -0.0002 m) and the gate would brake
    # on the floor for ever.
    depth_floor_h_min: 0.05
    #
    # depth_floor_clip_wide_m / _narrow_m — the two fit windows, in metres of
    # perpendicular distance to the CURRENT estimate of the plane. The wide first
    # pass is what buys pitch tolerance, and it is safe against large obstacles
    # for a geometric reason: it admits only the bottom 25 cm of a wall, and a
    # wall's bottom edge lies ON the floor plane.
    # ★ Both numbers are measured, not chosen. On real clouds with a synthetic
    #   wall injected over 30% of the points, this schedule lands within 0.3 cm of
    #   the reference, while unweighted iterative least squares is dragged 3.6 cm
    #   off (H 0.3099 -> 0.3462).
    # ★ A narrow-only schedule [0.06, 0.06] is what "just trust the prior"
    #   suggests, and it is wrong: measured, it loses the floor entirely at
    #   +15 deg of pitch error and biases H by +3.6 cm at -10 deg. That bias is
    #   the dangerous direction -- it lifts the plane, so the floor itself starts
    #   reading as an obstacle.
    depth_floor_clip_wide_m: 0.25
    depth_floor_clip_narrow_m: 0.06
    #
    # depth_floor_min_fit_points — below this the frame yields no floor model and
    # the reading becomes no_floor_model rather than clear. Measured: a healthy
    # frame puts ~265000 points inside the wide window.
    depth_floor_min_fit_points: 2000
    #
    # depth_low_obstacle_m — the discrepancy test. The lidar scans a horizontal
    #   plane at roughly 0.22 m; anything shorter is invisible to it and to the
    #   ultrasonics (0.25 m range, and soft/sloped surfaces often return
    #   nothing). The band does see it. A stop is raised only when the camera
    #   reports something closer than this AND the lidar, on the same bearing,
    #   sees nothing (or something farther by depth_lidar_margin_m) -- i.e. the
    #   two disagree. A bare threshold would instead fire on the floor and on
    #   everything the lidar already handles.
    # ★ CALIBRATED 2026-09-21 from a 1216-frame empty-corridor baseline (plus a
    #   second 185-frame run). The binding number is the NEAREST point noise can
    #   produce: 1.455 m over the first run, 1.524 m over the second. So any
    #   threshold below ~1.45 m cannot fire on noise.
    #   The baseline also showed where the noise lives, and it does not look like
    #   floor relief: never closer than z = 1.24 m, narrow in x (p50 span 2 cm),
    #   and mostly pinned at x ~ -0.70 m for z ~ 1.25 m -- which is exactly where
    #   the horizontal FOV ends. Field-edge artefact is the better explanation.
    #   1.00 keeps 0.455 m of margin and still detects out to ~1 m: a 0.10 m box
    #   at 0.565 m yields depth_used ~= 0.60 m, well inside it.
    #   ⚠️ 0.0 = TEST OFF. The POSITIVE half of this calibration (box at
    #   0.4/0.6/0.8/1.0 m, plan 11.4) was still outstanding when this was
    #   written -- see plan 11.5. Do not raise this value without re-running it.
    depth_low_obstacle_m: 1.00
    # depth_lidar_margin_m — how much farther than the camera the lidar must
    #   read before the two are called a disagreement. It has to exceed the
    #   systematic offset between the two sensors looking at the SAME object
    #   (measured 0.46..0.80 m when both see the box), or a real object would be
    #   misread as a lidar blind spot.
    #   Measured 2026-09-21, both directions: lidar front 1.376 m against depth
    #   0.60 m on the box -> disagreement (fires); 1.376 m against noise at
    #   >= 1.455 m -> agreement (does not). 0.15 separates the two cases.
    depth_lidar_margin_m: 0.15
    # ── Added 2026-09-20 (A1/A2). ──
    #
    # depth_ttl_sec — a frame older than this stops counting as evidence about
    #   the front. 0 disables the check. ★ CALIBRATED 2026-09-21 against the
    #   WORST-CASE interval the plan asked for, not the mean: over 1215 measured
    #   front_down inter-arrivals the mean was 0.148 s (6.75 Hz), p99.9 0.278 s,
    #   and the worst was 0.294 s, with nothing above 0.3 s at all. 0.7 is 2.4x
    #   that worst case.
    #   (History: this used to read 0.5 s, guessed from a presumed 10 fps, and
    #   then 0.7 s as ~3x the MEAN of a 4.31 Hz points_nav. Both were guesses.
    #   The frame rate above is measured on the raw front_down cloud, which is
    #   what the gate actually reads.)
    #   On 2026-09-20 this mattered for real: the bridge's subscriptions died at
    #   1789867268 and the gate kept acting on that last reading for hours,
    #   because the old reader had no timestamp and two fail-open branches that
    #   returned the stale value.
    #   Live-tunable, no restart: ros2 param set /xw_safety_gate depth_ttl_sec <s>
    depth_ttl_sec: 0.7
    #
    # nav_slowdown_band_m / nav_slowdown_floor_ratio — nav used to go straight
    #   from full speed to a hard zero at the stop threshold. It now ramps:
    #   ratio 1.0 at (stop_m + band), falling linearly to floor_ratio at stop_m.
    #   The band is measured from the sector's OWN stop_m, not from
    #   nav_safety_distance — a band hung off nav_safety_distance (0.28) would
    #   sit entirely inside the already-blocked region and could never produce a
    #   non-zero output.
    #   With depth/lidar stop_m 0.40: full speed at 1.00 m, floor from 0.40 m.
    #   Floor = 0.25 * max_linear_speed 0.45 = 0.1125 m/s, which is far above
    #   xw_cmd_arbiter's isActive() eps of 1e-3, so a slowdown is never mistaken
    #   for "this source is gone".
    #   A slowdown never sets safety_ok false — /safety_status keeps meaning
    #   "am I stopping", and "slowing" is a separate signal
    #   (/obstacle_status nav_slowdown).
    nav_slowdown_band_m: 0.60
    nav_slowdown_floor_ratio: 0.25
    enable_teleop_oa: true
    avoid_turn_speed: 0.35
    avoid_back_speed: 0.18
    max_linear_speed: 0.45
    max_angular_speed: 0.85
    enable_recharge_pass_through: true
    recharge_pass_linear_max: 0.08
